rn-media: teach node duplication about byte arrays (upstream backport)

WHY
    Every `MPV_FORMAT_NODE` property is copied out of the property handler
    through `copy_node()` -> `dup_node()`. In mpv 0.35.1 `dup_node`'s switch has
    no `MPV_FORMAT_BYTE_ARRAY` arm, so a byte array inside a node property falls
    into `default:` and the node is stamped `(mpv_format)-1` — invalid. The
    member survives the trip in name only.

    That is exactly what `pcm-tap-frame` (004.rn_media_pcm_tap.patch) hits: on
    device its scalars (`sample_rate`, `channels`, `frames`, `seq`) arrived
    intact while `samples` came through empty, which reads like a tap that has
    no audio rather than a copy that dropped a field. Verified 2026-08-11: the
    property itself reported `channels: 2, frames: 2048, seq: 1082` at the same
    moment the client saw no samples at all.

PROVENANCE
    Not our invention — this is upstream mpv's own fix, copied verbatim.
    `dup_node` gained the arm in **v0.36.0**, so:

      * the Android fork (mpv 0.35.1) needs this patch;
      * the darwin fork (mpv 0.36.0) does NOT — it already has the identical
        code, which is why the two forks carry the same 004 and only this one
        differs.

REBASING
    **Delete this file the moment the Android fork's mpv reaches 0.36.0.** It
    will fail to apply then, loudly, which is the outcome you want: the hunk it
    adds is already there.

diff --git a/options/m_option.c b/options/m_option.c
--- a/options/m_option.c
+++ b/options/m_option.c
@@ -3774,6 +3774,16 @@
         }
         break;
     }
+    case MPV_FORMAT_BYTE_ARRAY: {
+        struct mpv_byte_array *old = node->u.ba;
+        struct mpv_byte_array *new = talloc_zero(ta_parent, struct mpv_byte_array);
+        node->u.ba = new;
+        if (old->size > 0) {
+            *new = *old;
+            new->data = talloc_memdup(new, old->data, old->size);
+        }
+        break;
+    }
     case MPV_FORMAT_NONE:
     case MPV_FORMAT_FLAG:
     case MPV_FORMAT_INT64:
